  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM EST Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and tranquil weather \nthrough Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure remains overhead allowing for mild days and seasonably \ncool night. Some high clouds filtering into the area in the SW flow \naloft will likely keep overnight lows tonight higher than they've \nbeen the past few days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Ensemble guidance showing increasing chance for rain \nand gusty winds on Sunday, especially close to the coast.\n\nAs we've been monitoring for the last few days, low pressure is \nexpected to develop along the stationary frontal boundary across \nFlorida and the Bahamas. Still somewhat lower confidence for the \neventual intensity and track of expected coastal low development for \nthe weekend due to the uncertainty of where along the front the low \nwill develop. However, the super-ensemble of the 12z GEFS, 06z EPS \nand 06z AI-EPS suggest a potential low track a little closer to the \ncoast. The deterministic runs of the 12z GFS and 12z REFS show a \nstrong low moving onshore in the Carolinas, while the 12z ECMWF \nshows a slower and weaker low moving toward the Carolina coastline, \nthen moving northeast by Sunday night. \n\nAlthough the eventual track is still uncertain, there is somewhat \nincreased confidence that the low will be close enough to the region \nto allow for increased rain chances across the entire area. The best \nchance for rain will still be in eastern VA/NE NC, as the \nprobabilities for 1\" of rainfall across especially areas east of I-\n95 have increased to 50+ percent in the latest NBM guidance. With \nthat said, have increased PoPs across the entire area on Sunday. \nThere are still some scenarios which the area sees very little \nrainfall, but those scenarios are seemingly less likely than \nyesterday. \n\nAs for other impacts, there could be a brief period of gusty NE to \nSE winds along the coast on Sunday, but the system will be moving \nfast enough to limit any significant impacts. In addition, given \nthat the winds will likely be shifting and we will be coming out of \nthe lower end of the astronomical high tide cycle, only minor tidal \nflooding is expected in the worst case scenario.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for another storm system by Thanksgiving.\n\nCooler and dry conditions expected by Tuesday as strong Canadian \nhigh pressure builds across the northern states and New England. \nThere is an increasing signal of some type of low pressure \ndevelopment by Wednesday or Thanksgiving along the frontal boundary \nsouth of the area. This is still a week out and there will be plenty \nof time to pin down any details. However, given the +2 sigma high to \nthe north, any low pressure to our south will lead to increased NE \nwinds and the potential for some tidal flooding as we will be going \ninto another King Tide cycle.",
